// Original educational explanations, hypothetical examples and practice exercises.
// Order matches the existing five lesson outlines within each topic.
module.exports = {
stocks: [
  {
    sections: [['How the market is organized', 'A listed share can trade on several venues even though it has a primary listing exchange. Quotes show a bid and an ask; the difference is the spread. Displayed size describes the quantity available at a quoted price, not a promise that all of your order will fill there. Regular sessions, opening auctions and extended hours can have very different liquidity.'], ['Build an execution checklist', 'Before studying an entry, identify the ticker, listing venue, session and upcoming company events. Compare the spread with the price move your idea depends on. A market order prioritizes execution but can fill at several prices. A limit order sets a price constraint but may remain unfilled. Trading halts and overnight gaps can interrupt either plan.']],
    example: 'Suppose a stock is quoted at $49.98 bid and $50.02 ask. Buying and immediately selling 100 shares at those prices costs $4 in spread alone, before commissions or price movement.',
    exercise: 'Observe one liquid and one less liquid stock at the same time. Record bid, ask and displayed size. Explain which order would be more exposed to slippage and why.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Separate risk from buying power', 'Position sizing starts with the loss budget in your hypothetical plan, not the largest order a broker permits. Divide that budget by the distance between entry and the planned stop. For ordinary shares, that distance is the price risk per share before costs. Then compare the resulting purchase value with available cash or permitted margin.'], ['Allow for imperfect execution', 'Whole-share rules require rounding down. Fees and possible slippage consume part of the budget, while an overnight gap can make the actual loss larger than the stop-distance calculation. If a logical stop requires a position smaller than the minimum available size, the trade may not fit the plan. Widening the loss budget merely to keep the trade defeats the calculation.']],
    example: 'A $100 planned loss and a $2.50 stop distance give 40 shares before costs. At a $60 entry the position value is $2,400. Risk budget and capital required are different numbers.',
    exercise: 'Use a worksheet to compare two stop distances with the same risk budget. Divide the budget by each stop distance and explain why doubling the distance halves the calculated share quantity.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Separate the announcement from the reaction', 'An earnings release can contain revenue, profit, guidance and revisions to earlier expectations. A headline beat does not determine the next price move. Compare the reported period, accounting basis and outlook with what the market was expecting, then observe how price and liquidity actually respond.'], ['Define scenarios before the release', 'For a paper exercise, distinguish a gap that holds above the prior range from one that returns inside it. Write the condition that would invalidate each interpretation. If a position spans the announcement, an opening gap may skip a stop entirely. Options add another layer because implied volatility can fall after an event even when the stock moves in the anticipated direction.']],
    example: 'A stock closes at 100, opens at 108 after earnings and later trades at 103. It is up on the day, but a trader who bought at the opening price is losing before costs.',
    exercise: 'Read one company release from its investor-relations website. List three facts separately from three interpretations. Compare the opening move with the closing move without assuming a repeatable strategy.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Measure relative performance consistently', 'Sector rotation describes changes in which industries lead or lag a broader market. Start with a benchmark, a common measurement window and comparable return data. A sector can outperform while both it and the benchmark fall; relative strength alone does not establish a positive absolute return.'], ['Look through the sector label', 'Two sector funds can use different classification systems, weights and holdings. A large constituent can dominate a fund’s performance. Compare concentration, overlap, costs and earnings exposure before treating a sector as a single economic bet. Choosing the best performer after the period has finished creates hindsight bias; a research rule needs to specify the information available at the decision date.']],
    example: 'If a sector falls 2% while the benchmark falls 5%, its simple return difference is +3 percentage points. That is relative outperformance alongside an absolute loss.',
    exercise: 'Choose a fixed historical window and compare two sectors with one benchmark. Repeat for a different window and note whether the apparent leadership changes.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['What volume can and cannot reveal', 'Volume records traded quantity, not the identity or intention of the participants. Volume profile groups that activity by price; VWAP weights transaction prices by volume over a specified period. Both describe the selected data and session. Neither proves that an institution is accumulating or defending a level.'], ['Use consistent anchors and confirmation', 'Record the venue coverage, session boundary and anchor before interpreting a chart. Compare price acceptance around a level with subsequent trading rather than attributing every large print to informed buying. A VWAP calculated from an opening anchor differs from one anchored to an event. Historical reactions are useful observations for a journal, but they do not guarantee another reaction.']],
    example: 'Ten shares trading at $100 and 30 at $102 produce a VWAP of $101.50. The simple average of the two quoted prices is $101, which ignores their different volumes.',
    exercise: 'Calculate VWAP from a small table of trades, then change the starting point. Describe how the result changes without assigning a motive to the participants.'
  }
],
funds: [
  {
    sections: [['Separate the fund from its trading price', 'An exchange-traded fund holds a portfolio or follows an investment mandate while its shares trade on an exchange. Net asset value reflects the value of the fund’s assets minus liabilities per share. The exchange price can differ from that estimate, particularly when underlying markets are closed or less liquid.'], ['Read beyond the ticker', 'Inspect the objective, holdings, expense ratio, spread and the benchmark methodology. Two funds with similar names may provide different exposure. Tracking difference measures the return gap against a benchmark; tracking error describes variability in that gap. Neither is the same as the bid-ask spread paid when trading shares. Distributions also matter when comparing total returns.']],
    example: 'An ETF quoted at $100.10 against a $100.00 per-share NAV estimate trades at a 0.10% premium. Buying and later selling also involves the prevailing spread and any brokerage charges.',
    exercise: 'Compare the official factsheets of two funds with a similar objective. List one difference in holdings, costs and benchmark construction before comparing returns.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Define the role of each holding', 'A core-satellite framework separates broad exposure from smaller, more specialized positions. The terms describe portfolio roles, not universally correct percentages. A useful plan states the objective, time horizon and loss tolerance before selecting funds. A specialized fund can overlap heavily with a broad core despite having a different name.'], ['Make the rules observable', 'Specify how allocation drift will be measured and when it will be reviewed. Account for fees, taxes and the possibility that several satellites react to the same factor. A portfolio organized into many tickers can still be concentrated in a handful of companies or one industry. Rebalancing rules are easier to evaluate when written before a market move.']],
    example: 'In a hypothetical $10,000 portfolio, an 80% broad-market allocation is $8,000 and a 20% specialized allocation is $2,000. If both own the same largest companies, the combined concentration exceeds what the two labels suggest.',
    exercise: 'Draw an exposure map for a hypothetical three-fund portfolio. Identify overlapping holdings and explain what each fund adds to the stated objective.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Define the regime using available evidence', 'A market regime is a research label for conditions such as growth, inflation or volatility. The label is not directly observable in advance. Define the indicators, thresholds and publication lag before studying a rotation rule, and use only data that would have been available on each decision date.'], ['Test costs and alternative explanations', 'A rotation backtest can look persuasive when it chooses favorable dates or uses revised economic data. Include turnover, spreads and fund expenses, then compare the result with a simpler benchmark. Examine losing periods and what happens when the regime classification is wrong. A macro narrative can explain a past return without providing a reliable timing rule.']],
    example: 'If a rule uses a monthly inflation release published in February, it cannot legitimately rebalance on January 31 using that future observation. The tradable decision date comes after publication.',
    exercise: 'Write a paper rotation rule with one indicator, a publication delay and a fixed review interval. Identify the transaction costs and revisions the test would need to handle.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Identify the actual instrument', 'A volatility-linked product may hold futures or use derivatives rather than own the spot volatility index. Its objective can be daily, leveraged or inverse. Read the prospectus to identify the benchmark, reset period and whether the product is a fund or an unsecured exchange-traded note.'], ['Understand the path of returns', 'Rolling futures changes exposure as contracts approach expiration. The shape of the futures curve can create a return drag or benefit that differs from changes in the spot index. Daily leverage also means multi-day returns depend on the path, not just the starting and ending level. These features make a volatility product very different from a simple long-term hedge.']],
    example: 'A hypothetical daily 2× product rises 20% when its benchmark rises 10%. If the benchmark then falls 10%, the product falls 20% from its new value: 100 becomes 120 and then 96, before costs.',
    exercise: 'Locate the reset period and benchmark in one product’s official documents. Explain why a multi-day return need not equal a fixed multiple of the spot VIX change.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Compare the index rules', 'Smart-beta funds apply a rules-based weighting scheme, such as value, quality, size or low volatility. The factor label is only a starting point. Inspect which metrics are used, how often the fund rebalances and whether sector or constituent limits constrain the result.'], ['Inspect unintended exposures', 'A value screen can concentrate in particular industries, and a low-volatility screen can still experience substantial losses. Compare actual holdings, turnover, capacity, costs and overlap with existing positions. Historical factor premiums can disappear for long periods. A fund’s recent outperformance may reflect one sector or a few holdings rather than a durable factor advantage.']],
    example: 'Two funds called “quality” might use profitability alone or combine profitability with debt and earnings stability. They can therefore select different companies and react differently to changing financing conditions.',
    exercise: 'Read two index methodology summaries for the same factor label. Write the selection and weighting differences in plain language and identify one concentration risk.'
  }
],
futures: [
  {
    sections: [['Read the contract specification first', 'A futures contract specifies an underlying exposure, multiplier, minimum price increment, expiration and settlement process. Contract symbols can look similar while representing different sizes. Cash-settled contracts and physically delivered contracts create different obligations as expiration approaches.'], ['Convert price movement into currency', 'Tick value equals tick size multiplied by the contract multiplier for a simple linear contract. Planned risk then depends on ticks to the stop and the number of contracts. Check the exchange specification and your broker’s rules rather than inferring the multiplier from the symbol. Session breaks, price limits and rollover dates also affect execution.']],
    example: 'A hypothetical contract with a 0.25-point tick and a $20-per-point multiplier has a $5 tick value. Eight ticks across three contracts represent $120 of price risk before fees and slippage.',
    exercise: 'Create a contract card containing multiplier, tick size, tick value, expiration and settlement type. Recalculate the same stop for a smaller contract version using its actual specification.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Margin is a performance bond', 'Futures margin supports the obligations of a leveraged contract; it is not the full purchase price of the underlying exposure. Initial and maintenance requirements can differ, and a broker may impose additional or intraday requirements. Changes in market volatility can lead to higher requirements.'], ['Stress the account, not only the entry', 'Notional exposure comes from contract price, multiplier and quantity. Profit and loss can be large relative to the margin posted. Compare an adverse price scenario with account equity, cash needs and other positions. A low intraday margin offer does not make the position economically small, and losses can exceed the amount initially deposited.']],
    example: 'A hypothetical $100,000 notional position supported by $5,000 of margin has 20 times as much notional as posted margin. A 2% adverse move represents roughly $2,000 for a linear exposure before costs.',
    exercise: 'For a paper position, list notional exposure, initial margin, maintenance margin and a stressed loss separately. Explain which number changes when the broker raises its requirement.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Use session labels as hypotheses', 'An opening drive describes sustained directional movement near a session open. A balance day describes more two-sided trading within a range. These are observations that develop over time, not facts known at the first tick. Define the opening interval and the evidence needed before applying either label.'], ['Record invalidation and selection bias', 'An early breakout can reverse, while a quiet opening can precede a trend. For research, record every qualifying session rather than only memorable examples. Distinguish the cash-market open from the futures session boundary, and note scheduled announcements. Comparing different opening definitions without controlling them can produce misleading conclusions.']],
    example: 'Price moves above the first 15-minute range and then closes back inside it. That observation weakens a simple breakout interpretation, but it does not by itself prove that the rest of the day will remain balanced.',
    exercise: 'Label ten historical sessions using one fixed rule and only information available at the time. Record ambiguous cases and the cost of any assumed entries.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Distinguish an order from an outcome', 'A stop order is activated when its trigger condition is met; the execution that follows depends on order type, liquidity and venue rules. A stop-market order can slip. A stop-limit order constrains price but may fail to execute if the market moves through the limit.'], ['Plan for discontinuous prices', 'Fast moves can widen spreads, consume displayed liquidity and trigger exchange limits. Compare your intended size with the contract’s tick value and plausible slippage. A research plan should state what happens if the position cannot be exited immediately. Increasing distance after entry changes the original loss budget unless size is reduced or the plan is reassessed.']],
    example: 'A hypothetical stop triggers at 100 but fills at 98.5. With a $20-per-point multiplier and two contracts, that 1.5-point difference adds $60 of loss beyond the trigger-price estimate.',
    exercise: 'Compare stop-market and stop-limit outcomes in a price-gap scenario. Write the execution risk each order type retains rather than calling either one risk-free.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Explain the economic link', 'Cross-market research compares instruments that may respond to shared drivers, such as growth, inflation or funding conditions. A relationship needs an economic explanation and a defined time window. The same stock and bond markets can move together under one shock and in opposite directions under another.'], ['Align data before drawing conclusions', 'Use synchronized timestamps, comparable returns and the correct contract month. A delayed quote or a rolling futures series can create a false divergence. Confirmation should be an additional observation, not an excuse to ignore the original invalidation level. Several correlated positions can also multiply one underlying risk rather than diversify it.']],
    example: 'A long equity-index position and a short government-bond position may both be exposed to a growth narrative. A policy surprise can change both legs at once even though the tickers belong to different asset classes.',
    exercise: 'Choose a historical event and compare aligned returns across three markets. State the original hypothesis, an alternative explanation and a condition that would contradict it.'
  }
],
forex: [
  {
    sections: [['Read the quote in the correct direction', 'A currency pair expresses the price of a base currency in units of a quote currency. EUR/USD at 1.10 means one euro is quoted at 1.10 US dollars. Buying the pair creates long exposure to the base and short exposure to the quote currency. Account-currency conversion is a separate step.'], ['Compare execution conditions', 'Pairs differ in typical spread, liquidity, session activity and sensitivity to announcements. A tight displayed spread can widen around a release or rollover. Contract size and pip conventions also differ, so verify the broker specification before translating a price move into money. “Major” or “minor” is a classification, not a promise of safe execution.']],
    example: 'For 10,000 EUR in EUR/USD, a move from 1.1000 to 1.1010 changes the quote-currency value by $10. That is ten conventional 0.0001 pips before fees.',
    exercise: 'Write the base, quote, conventional pip size and account conversion for two pairs. Explain how a stronger US dollar could affect their quoted prices differently.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Separate local time from session time', 'Forex is decentralized, and session labels describe broad regional activity rather than one universal exchange schedule. London and New York business hours overlap, but their daylight-saving changes occur on different dates. A fixed UTC schedule can therefore be wrong for part of the year.'], ['Observe rather than assume a pattern', 'Liquidity and realized movement can vary around scheduled releases, holidays and market openings. Compare the same pair over the same observation window before concluding that a session is consistently more active. An overlap does not ensure direction or profitability. Spreads, transaction costs and the time required to manage a position belong in the study.']],
    example: 'A release scheduled for 08:30 in New York retains its local release time while its UTC equivalent changes with daylight saving. Your own local time may change on a different date.',
    exercise: 'Use the market-hours page to compare New York and London in two different months. Record the timezone labels and check a holiday before building a paper schedule.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Give each timeframe a specific job', 'A multi-timeframe study separates broader context from execution detail. A daily chart might describe a range, an hourly chart a local structure, and a shorter chart the event used to test an entry hypothesis. The selected intervals should match the holding period and remain consistent through the study.'], ['Avoid counting one move several times', 'A daily breakout and an hourly breakout may describe the same underlying price movement. They are not independent confirmations. Define what would invalidate the idea and measure the risk from the actual entry to that level. Looking at more timeframes until one agrees introduces selection bias and makes the rule difficult to evaluate.']],
    example: 'A market can rise within an hourly range while remaining inside a larger daily downtrend. Both descriptions can be true; the intended holding period determines which hypothesis is being tested.',
    exercise: 'Annotate one historical chart at three fixed intervals. Write one observation per interval, then a single invalidation rule without changing the intervals after seeing the outcome.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Identify the release and its baseline', 'Economic announcements can change expected interest rates and currency valuations, but reactions depend on expectations and positioning. Check the exact event, reference period, timezone and possible revisions. A calendar’s impact label is a summary, not a measurement of the move that will occur.'], ['Plan execution uncertainty explicitly', 'During a release, available liquidity can shrink and spreads can widen. A stop can fill beyond its trigger, and an initial move can reverse when additional details are read. A paper plan can compare staying flat, reducing exposure or waiting for conditions to settle; none should be described as a guaranteed way to profit from the announcement.']],
    example: 'A jobs headline exceeds the published consensus while the prior month is revised lower. Looking only at the new headline leaves out information that may matter to the market response.',
    exercise: 'Open an official release and list the headline, revision and next scheduled publication. Write a no-trade condition for a hypothetical plan, such as an unacceptable spread.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Map the currencies behind the tickers', 'Several currency pairs can share the same underlying exposure. Long EUR/USD and long GBP/USD both include a short-US-dollar component. Different pair names therefore do not establish diversification. Summing planned stop losses is useful, but it does not capture a common gap or a changing correlation.'], ['Measure a relationship with care', 'Compare returns over aligned timestamps and use an appropriate quote direction. Reversing a currency pair changes the interpretation. Correlation is estimated from a chosen sample and can shift sharply around policy events. A risk review should include both the observed relationship and a stress scenario in which positions move against the account together.']],
    example: 'Two paper positions each budget $50 of stop risk, for $100 combined. A single dollar shock can affect both, while slippage can push actual losses beyond the combined estimate.',
    exercise: 'Create a currency exposure table for three pairs. Identify repeated long and short currencies, then compare it with a return-correlation matrix over more than one window.'
  }
],
crypto: [
  {
    sections: [['A continuous market still has interruptions', 'Crypto trading runs across many venues around the clock, but liquidity and access are not constant. Exchanges can pause trading or withdrawals, stablecoins can deviate from their reference value, and an instrument’s price can differ across venues. A chart should identify the exchange and the quote asset.'], ['Observe liquidity at the intended size', 'The spread and order-book depth matter more than a headline volume number when estimating an execution. Weekend conditions can differ from weekday conditions, but the effect varies by asset and venue. Record depth, spread and realized movement together. Large price changes can occur when fewer orders are available to absorb a trade.']],
    example: 'A token quoted at 10.00 on one venue and 10.15 on another does not establish a risk-free opportunity. Fees, withdrawal restrictions, transfer time and available depth can consume the apparent difference.',
    exercise: 'Compare the same asset on two venues using the same quote currency and timestamp. Note spread and depth as well as the last traded price.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Identify the exposure and obligations', 'A spot position generally involves holding an asset or a claim on it with a custodian. A perpetual derivative provides contract exposure without a fixed ordinary expiration, subject to the venue’s rules. Funding payments can flow between long and short holders, and collateral supports leveraged positions.'], ['Check the margin model', 'Isolated and cross margin allocate collateral differently. Liquidation can use a mark price rather than the last traded price, and maintenance requirements may increase with position size. Positive funding does not guarantee that its sign or magnitude will persist. The product may also be unavailable or restricted in some jurisdictions.']],
    example: 'A hypothetical $5,000 linear perpetual position with a 0.01% funding payment incurs $0.50 for that interval if the position is on the paying side. Repeated intervals and changing rates alter the total.',
    exercise: 'Compare a spot holding and a derivative with the same notional exposure. List custody, funding, collateral and liquidation differences using the venue’s own product rules.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Know what the metric measures', 'On-chain data records activity on a blockchain, but interpreting ownership often relies on labels and estimation. A transfer to an exchange-labeled address may be an internal reorganization rather than a sale. Coverage, entity attribution and later revisions can materially change a published metric.'], ['Separate observation from interpretation', 'Read the provider’s methodology and timestamp, then compare several possible explanations. Stablecoin issuance, active addresses and exchange balances measure different activity and are not direct measures of new buying demand. Avoid treating a visually compelling chart as independent evidence when several metrics are derived from the same transactions.']],
    example: 'An exchange moves 1,000 units from one known wallet to another. A dataset with incomplete address labels could report an inflow even though customer demand has not changed.',
    exercise: 'Choose an on-chain metric and write its definition, data coverage and two alternative explanations for a spike. Identify what additional evidence would distinguish them.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Define a theme before measuring performance', 'Narrative rotation describes shifting attention among groups of crypto assets. Group membership is subjective and can change after prices rise. Define the assets and the observation period before calculating returns; otherwise a theme assembled from recent winners exaggerates its historical success.'], ['Inspect liquidity and shared dependencies', 'Assets associated with a theme can depend on the same exchange, collateral asset or funding conditions. Attention does not establish cash flow, adoption or durable value. Compare market structure, token supply events and executable liquidity alongside the story. A paper watchlist should record why an asset was included before observing its subsequent return.']],
    example: 'A five-token basket that excludes a failed token after the fact shows a better return than the basket a trader could actually have held. That is survivorship bias, even if the theme sounds convincing.',
    exercise: 'Create a dated hypothetical theme list with fixed inclusion rules. Record delistings and losing assets as well as winners when reviewing it later.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Consider a chain of stressed events', 'A crypto selloff can combine falling collateral value, higher margin pressure and thinner liquidity. Forced selling can amplify a move, but a liquidation chart does not predict exactly when that will occur. Correlations estimated during calm periods may understate the possibility of several holdings falling together.'], ['Write operational assumptions down', 'A paper stress test should include price gaps, spread widening, unavailable exchange access and changes in collateral value. A stop order cannot protect against every venue outage. Account for where assets are held and whether positions share collateral. Reducing a position after a shock may be much more expensive than the pre-event quote suggests.']],
    example: 'If a $10,000 asset exposure falls 15%, the price loss is $1,500 before costs. If its collateral also falls in value, margin pressure can worsen beyond that simple calculation.',
    exercise: 'Stress a hypothetical account with an asset decline, a collateral decline and a temporary inability to trade. Identify which assumptions a basic liquidation calculator cannot represent.'
  }
],
indices: [
  {
    sections: [['Distinguish a benchmark from a product', 'An index is a calculated measure built from a set of constituents and weighting rules. It is not itself the same as an ETF, futures contract or other product linked to it. Price-return and total-return versions differ because the latter accounts for distributions according to the methodology.'], ['Read the concentration', 'A capitalization-weighted index can be driven by its largest constituents. Equal-weighted versions can behave differently even with similar membership. Check rebalancing rules, sector exposure and currency before using an index as a market proxy. A broad index rising does not mean that most individual securities are rising.']],
    example: 'In a two-company illustration, a company weighted 80% rises 5% while the company weighted 20% falls 5%. The weighted index rises about 3% even though one of its two members falls.',
    exercise: 'Compare a price-weighted, capitalization-weighted and equal-weighted methodology. Explain what a large price move in one constituent would mean in each.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Fix the definition of the range', 'An opening range is the high and low over a chosen interval after a specified opening time. It might use the cash-market open or another session boundary. The interval, data source and treatment of premarket activity must be defined before testing a breakout idea.'], ['Measure failures as carefully as successes', 'A move outside the range can reverse immediately. Record entry rules, invalidation, slippage and scheduled releases rather than choosing only clean examples. A wider opening range can imply a larger stop distance, which changes the quantity allowed by a fixed loss budget. The range is an observation, not proof of an edge.']],
    example: 'If the first 30 minutes span 100 to 102, a later trade at 102.10 is above the observed range. Whether it qualifies as a signal depends on a prewritten rule, such as a closing price rather than an intrabar touch.',
    exercise: 'Compare touch-based and close-based rules on the same historical sessions. Include false breakouts and costs without changing the rule after seeing results.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Measure participation', 'Market breadth asks how widely a move is shared. An advance-decline count, the proportion above a moving average and sector participation each measure different things. Define the security universe and avoid comparing a broad exchange statistic with a much narrower index as if their memberships were identical.'], ['Treat divergences as context', 'An index can rise while participation narrows because its largest constituents have strong returns. That divergence may be useful context, but it does not specify the date of a reversal. Check changes in index membership and the handling of missing data before comparing history. Several breadth measures built from the same prices are not independent observations.']],
    example: 'An index rises while 40 constituents advance and 60 decline. The count signals narrow participation, but the index return also depends on the weights and size of each move.',
    exercise: 'For one historical date, compare index return, advancing share and sector returns. Explain what each observation adds and what it cannot tell you about the next session.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Separate the decision from expectations', 'Index markets can react to policy statements, forecasts and press conferences as well as a headline rate decision. An unchanged rate can still produce a large move if expectations change. Identify the release sequence and official timezone before studying an event day.'], ['Plan several observable outcomes', 'Write hypotheses for stronger, weaker or mixed information, then describe how each could be contradicted. The first price move may reverse during later commentary. Order-book depth and spreads can change suddenly. A paper plan should state acceptable execution conditions and whether it assumes holding through the event or waiting afterward.']],
    example: 'A policy rate is unchanged, but the accompanying projections imply a different future path. The market response may reflect the new path rather than the unchanged current rate.',
    exercise: 'Build an event timeline from the official publisher. Separate the statement, projections and press conference, then mark where a historical price reaction changed direction.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Keep volatility measures distinct', 'Implied volatility reflects option prices and a model; realized volatility describes observed returns. A volatility index is not a direct prediction of market direction. ATR measures price range in the units of the instrument and is different from annualized return volatility.'], ['Compare like-for-like windows', 'A position plan may use volatility to explore different stop distances or exposure scenarios, but any sizing rule needs explicit assumptions. An increase in volatility can occur after losses have already happened. Historical calm does not justify ignoring event risk, and low volatility does not imply that a leveraged position is safe.']],
    example: 'If an illustrative ATR doubles from 10 to 20 points, a fixed two-ATR stop doubles from 20 to 40 points. Keeping the same loss budget would halve a simple linear position size before rounding and costs.',
    exercise: 'Compare an implied measure and a realized measure over the same event. State their units, lookback or horizon, and why their readings need not agree.'
  }
],
bonds: [
  {
    sections: [['Discount future cash flows', 'A conventional fixed-rate bond promises specified coupon payments and principal at maturity, subject to the issuer meeting its obligations. Its market price reflects the discounted value of those cash flows. When the required yield rises, the present value generally falls; when the required yield falls, the price generally rises.'], ['Distinguish yield measures', 'Coupon rate, current yield and yield to maturity answer different questions. Yield to maturity depends on contractual cash flows and assumptions about holding and reinvestment; it is not a guarantee of the realized return. Credit changes, embedded options, accrued interest and transaction costs can all matter alongside interest rates.']],
    example: 'A hypothetical one-year zero-coupon bond paying $1,000 is worth about $952.38 at a 5% required yield: $1,000 ÷ 1.05. At 6%, the same payment is worth about $943.40.',
    exercise: 'Recalculate the one-year example at two yields. Explain why the promised payment is unchanged even though the market value changes.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Use the correct duration concept', 'Modified duration estimates a bond’s percentage price sensitivity to a small change in yield, holding other assumptions constant. Macaulay duration measures a cash-flow-weighted time. Effective duration can account for changing cash flows in instruments with embedded options. These measures are related but are not interchangeable.'], ['Know the approximation’s limits', 'For a small parallel yield change, approximate percentage price change is negative modified duration multiplied by the yield change in decimal form. Convexity becomes more relevant for larger moves, while credit spread changes and a reshaping yield curve add further effects. A duration figure alone does not describe default or liquidity risk.']],
    example: 'A bond with modified duration 5 facing a 0.50 percentage-point yield increase has an approximate price change of −5 × 0.005 = −2.5%, before convexity and other changes.',
    exercise: 'Compare hypothetical durations of 2 and 8 under the same small yield move. State the assumptions that make the comparison useful and one reason actual returns could differ.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Compare yields across maturities', 'A yield curve places yields from comparable instruments against their maturities. The slope between two points is a spread, often expressed in basis points. Steepening means that spread widens; flattening means it narrows. Either can happen while both yields rise or while both fall.'], ['Avoid a one-variable forecast', 'Curve shapes reflect expectations, term premiums, supply and demand, among other influences. A historical relationship with growth does not give a precise timing signal. Compare like issuers and conventions, and identify which maturities are moving. A two-point spread leaves out changes in the middle of the curve.']],
    example: 'A two-year yield of 4.0% and ten-year yield of 4.5% create a +50-basis-point spread. If the two-year rises to 4.4% while the ten-year stays at 4.5%, the spread flattens to +10 basis points.',
    exercise: 'Write three yield scenarios: a parallel shift, a steepening and a flattening. Identify how a short-duration and a long-duration holding could respond differently.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Read the definition before the surprise', 'Inflation releases can report monthly or annual changes, headline or narrower measures, and seasonally adjusted or unadjusted data. CPI and PCE use different methods and coverage. Check the exact series and reference period before comparing an observation with expectations.'], ['Separate rate and credit effects', 'A surprise may alter expected policy rates, but bond returns also depend on duration, credit spreads and the price already paid. The same release can affect short and long maturities differently. Data revisions and details beneath the headline may alter the initial interpretation, while execution conditions can deteriorate around publication.']],
    example: 'An annual inflation rate can decline even when the monthly price level rises, because the comparison with the previous year changes. “Lower inflation” does not necessarily mean falling prices.',
    exercise: 'Read a publisher’s inflation table. Identify the monthly change, annual change and seasonal-adjustment basis, then write two plausible rate-market interpretations without assuming either is certain.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Identify the reason yields moved', 'Higher yields can reflect stronger expected growth, higher inflation, increased supply or a changing risk premium. Those causes can have different implications for equities, currencies and gold. A chart of two prices moving together does not identify the driver by itself.'], ['Compare exposures and timing', 'Rate-sensitive assets differ in cash flows, financing needs and currency exposure. A stock’s response depends on changes in expected earnings as well as discount rates. Currency returns involve the relative outlook between economies. Use aligned observation windows and separate an immediate reaction from a longer repricing.']],
    example: 'A rise in yields driven by stronger growth can coincide with improving earnings expectations. A rise driven by inflation pressure without growth may produce a different equity response, even if the yield change is similar.',
    exercise: 'Compare two historical yield increases. For each, list evidence about growth, inflation and policy expectations, then describe why other asset returns differed.'
  }
],
economy: [
  {
    sections: [['Build a small, traceable dataset', 'A macro dashboard is useful when each observation has a definition, reference period, release date and source. Growth, inflation, labor conditions and policy describe different parts of an economy. Mixing a fresh monthly release with an old annual observation without labels can create a false impression of current conditions.'], ['Keep observations and judgments separate', 'Record the published value and any revisions first, then write your interpretation in another field. A good dashboard also shows the next update and how missing data is handled. Adding many closely related indicators can create the illusion of confirmation while repeating the same information. Keep the research question explicit.']],
    example: 'A dashboard row reading “GDP: 3%” is ambiguous. A useful row identifies the country, quarter, whether the figure is annualized or year-on-year, the release date and the publisher.',
    exercise: 'Create four rows for growth, inflation, labor and policy. Include source and timestamps, then identify which observations can be directly compared and which cannot.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Measure the gap from expectations', 'A data surprise compares a published observation with an expectation collected before the release. The consensus is a survey summary, not a price-implied probability or a guarantee that all participants expected the same number. The surprise also depends on units and the dispersion of forecasts.'], ['Read the rest of the release', 'Revisions, subcomponents and simultaneous publications can change the message of the headline. Positioning and the policy backdrop affect the response. A numerically large surprise does not ensure a persistent trend, and the first trade after publication may reflect limited liquidity rather than a settled interpretation.']],
    example: 'A growth release of 2.0% against a 1.5% consensus is a +0.5-percentage-point surprise on the same basis. Calling that “0.5% growth” confuses a surprise with the reported growth rate.',
    exercise: 'Take a historical release and list actual, pre-release consensus, prior observation and revision. Explain which facts were available before publication and which arrived afterward.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Write conditions that can be checked', 'Scenario planning describes possible outcomes before an event. Define what “higher,” “lower” and “in line” mean in the units of the chosen data. Include a mixed-information scenario, since a headline can conflict with revisions or details. The purpose is to organize reasoning, not predict an exact price path.'], ['Connect scenarios to risk constraints', 'For a paper plan, define the observation that would invalidate each interpretation, acceptable execution conditions and whether taking no action is an outcome. Avoid assigning probabilities without evidence. Afterward, compare what actually occurred with the original notes rather than rewriting the plan to match the result.']],
    example: 'A headline inflation reading is higher than expected, but a closely watched underlying measure is lower. A plan with only “hot means sell” and “cool means buy” misses this mixed case.',
    exercise: 'Create a three-row scenario table for one upcoming release using hypothetical outcomes. Add an explicit “unclear information or poor liquidity” condition.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Distinguish policy instruments and communication', 'A central bank can change a policy rate, adjust its balance sheet or communicate a future stance. These actions need not move together. Read the official decision and the stated conditions rather than inferring a complete policy cycle from one phrase or one market move.'], ['Compare policy with expectations', 'Markets respond to changes in the expected path as well as the current setting. A cut can be less accommodative than investors expected, while an unchanged decision can carry more accommodative guidance. Economic conditions and the reason for the action matter. “Risk-on” and “risk-off” are broad descriptions, not rules that all assets must follow.']],
    example: 'If a hypothetical market expected a 50-basis-point cut and the bank delivers 25, the actual rate falls but the surprise is smaller easing than expected. These are two different descriptions of the same event.',
    exercise: 'Read an official policy statement and separate the action, economic assessment and forward conditions. Compare it with the previous statement without inventing a certainty about future decisions.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Translate a thesis into a testable exposure', 'A macro thesis is a claim about an economic mechanism. Turning it into a paper trade requires an instrument, time horizon and a reason that the view is not already fully reflected in price. Several instruments can express a similar idea but have different carry, liquidity, leverage and event risks.'], ['Write the ways the thesis can fail', 'Separate economic invalidation from a price-based exit and a time limit. A thesis can be directionally right but lose money because of entry price, timing or costs. Review the observed evidence after the event and record what would change your view. A persuasive story is not a substitute for a measurable position plan.']],
    example: 'A view that inflation will ease does not uniquely determine a bond, equity or currency position. Each expression also depends on growth, expectations, valuation and the horizon over which the view plays out.',
    exercise: 'Write one macro hypothesis and two hypothetical instruments that could express it. Compare their carry, risk drivers and falsification conditions before selecting a paper example.'
  }
],
options: [
  {
    sections: [['Read the contract terms', 'A call gives its holder a contractual right to buy; a put gives a right to sell, subject to the product’s terms. Strike, expiration, exercise style, settlement and multiplier all matter. Standard US equity contracts commonly reference 100 shares, but adjusted and other contracts can differ.'], ['Separate rights from obligations', 'A buyer pays a premium for the right. A writer accepts obligations and may face assignment under the contract’s rules. An option’s market value before expiration includes more than its immediate exercise value. An expiration payoff calculation therefore does not forecast what the option will trade for tomorrow.']],
    example: 'A hypothetical long call with a 50 strike and a 2 premium needs an underlying expiration price above 52 to show a profit before fees. At 51 it has exercise value but still loses 1 per unit.',
    exercise: 'Find strike, expiration, multiplier, exercise style and settlement for one contract. Explain what owning it and writing it would each require.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Understand the combined position', 'A vertical spread combines long and short options of the same type and expiration at different strikes. The combined expiration payoff can have a bounded gain and loss when the legs remain intact. That payoff definition does not remove execution, early assignment or expiration-management risk.'], ['Check the cash flows and leg handling', 'A debit spread pays a net premium; a credit spread receives one. Include contract multipliers and fees, and inspect what happens if only one leg is filled or assigned. Closing one leg changes the risk. Product exercise and settlement rules determine whether delivery or cash obligations can arise.']],
    example: 'A hypothetical 100/105 call debit spread purchased for 2 per unit has a maximum expiration loss of 2 and maximum gain of 3 per unit before costs, assuming the spread is intact and settled as modeled.',
    exercise: 'Draw the expiration payoff below, between and above the strikes. Then describe one operational event that the payoff drawing does not capture.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Greeks describe local sensitivities', 'Delta estimates sensitivity to the underlying, gamma describes how delta changes, theta relates to time passing, and vega relates to implied volatility. They are model estimates around the current state. Their units and signs depend on the provider and position direction, so check the convention before aggregating them.'], ['Recalculate as conditions change', 'A large move can change the sensitivities themselves. Holding other inputs constant helps interpret one Greek but is rarely a complete description of a real session. A position may gain from direction and lose from volatility or time at the same time. Short and long positions invert exposures, while a portfolio combines them across contracts.']],
    example: 'A long option with delta 0.40 has a first-order estimate of +0.40 per unit for a 1-unit underlying rise, holding other inputs fixed. Gamma and changes in volatility or time can make the actual price change different.',
    exercise: 'For a hypothetical contract, list the sign and unit of each Greek. Explain why delta alone cannot estimate the full outcome of an earnings announcement.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Implied volatility is embedded in price', 'Implied volatility is the model input consistent with an observed option price, given the other assumptions. It varies across strikes and expirations. Comparing its current level with history requires the same measure and a defined window; “high” relative to a quiet period need not mean an option is overpriced.'], ['Match the full risk profile', 'Buying or selling premium creates exposure to direction, volatility, time and tail events. A post-event fall in implied volatility can offset a favorable underlying move for a long option. Selling premium can incur large losses when realized moves exceed expectations. Strategy labels do not establish suitability or positive expected returns.']],
    example: 'A stock rises after earnings while a call loses value because the decline in implied volatility and time value outweighs the directional benefit. An expiration-only payoff chart cannot explain that change by itself.',
    exercise: 'Compare two expirations around an event. State what additional assumptions would be needed before calling either premium cheap or expensive.'
  },
  {
    sections: [['Aggregate with units and direction intact', 'Portfolio options risk combines quantity, contract multiplier and long or short sign. Net delta is one useful measure, but exposures to different underlyings are not directly comparable without a common risk model. A delta-neutral snapshot can still retain gamma, volatility, liquidity and event risk.'], ['Stress changes beyond a small move', 'Evaluate several underlying moves, volatility changes and time steps. Pay attention to concentrated expirations, correlated underlyings and short positions that can require margin or delivery. The Greeks change as markets move, and offsets that look effective in a small local estimate may weaken during a large shock.']],
    example: 'Two long calls at 0.40 delta and a 100 multiplier represent approximately +80 share-equivalent delta. Another position can offset that delta without offsetting the same gamma or vega.',
    exercise: 'Build a hypothetical two-position risk table with quantity, multiplier, delta, gamma and vega. Describe a scenario in which net delta is near zero but the portfolio still loses.'
  }
]
};
